{
  "track": "es",
  "level": "A1",
  "unit": 52,
  "title": "Big numbers",
  "description": "Count from fifteen to seventy the way Spanish builds them.",
  "explanation": {
    "summary": "Fifteen to nineteen are each one word, and thirty to seventy are single words you stack the small numbers after. This unit finishes every number you need below eighty.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Fifteen to nineteen",
        "body": "Quince, dieciséis, diecisiete, dieciocho and diecinueve. Each is one word, and only dieciséis keeps its accent. You met veintiún in unit 5; these are built the same way, ten plus the small number.",
        "examples": [
          {
            "es": "quince días",
            "en": "fifteen days"
          },
          {
            "es": "dieciséis años",
            "en": "sixteen years old"
          },
          {
            "es": "diecinueve euros",
            "en": "nineteen euros"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Thirty to seventy",
        "body": "Treinta, cuarenta, cincuenta, sesenta and setenta never change, whatever follows them. Treinta y dos is thirty-two, and the y is the only thing between the ten and the unit.",
        "examples": [
          {
            "es": "treinta y dos",
            "en": "thirty-two"
          },
          {
            "es": "cuarenta sillas",
            "en": "forty chairs"
          },
          {
            "es": "sesenta euros",
            "en": "sixty euros"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
